WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:08.392
Recently, many stores have started to offer organic food products, meaning those that are grown or made without the use of artificial chemicals.

2
00:00:08.393 --> 00:00:16.515
Although organic farmers do use fertilizers and pesticides on their crops, the chemicals are natural and used in lower quantities.

3
00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:22.585
As a result, organic methods produce foods that are healthier for the environment and for humans.

4
00:00:22.586 --> 00:00:33.057
Even though organic foods have only recently become popular, growing crops organically is not a modern development; organic farming is the oldest kind of agriculture in the world.

5
00:00:33.058 --> 00:00:42.432
Natural chemicals have been used by farmers to produce food for thousands of years, whereas artificial chemicals were not used on farms until the early twentieth century.

6
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:52.084
More than 6,000 years ago, people began to cultivate vegetables and grains in the Fertile Crescent, the area in the Middle East where civilization first developed.

7
00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:58.902
Over time, they discovered that if they added human or animal dung to the soil, their crops would produce higher yields.

8
00:00:58.903 --> 00:01:05.807
Meanwhile, in China, farmers kept ducks and fish in their rice fields to eat weeds and help fertilize the rice plants.

9
00:01:05.808 --> 00:01:10.287
These methods were used until the twentieth century all over the world.

10
00:01:10.288 --> 00:01:12.121
The Green Revolution

11
00:01:12.122 --> 00:01:19.463
After World War II, agriculture underwent a massive change, which the media called the Green Revolution.

12
00:01:19.464 --> 00:01:29.458
Irrigation technology was greatly improved, new farm machinery became commonplace, and scientists introduced hybrid crops that could grow better and faster.

13
00:01:29.459 --> 00:01:35.617
Farmers also began to use artificial fertilizers and pesticides to help them improve yields.

14
00:01:35.618 --> 00:01:44.095
This trend initially started in the United States, where chemical companies turned their attention to growing food to deal with rising demand from the growing population.

15
00:01:44.096 --> 00:01:50.287
To do this, scientists studied what kinds of chemicals plants require for robust growth.

16
00:01:50.288 --> 00:01:57.104
They discovered that most of these could be produced synthetically and applied to the fields to boost the quality of the soil.

17
00:01:57.105 --> 00:02:05.699
Nitrogen, for example, is essential for the survival of a plant and can be created artificially and sprayed onto the soil as a liquid.

18
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.699
Other nutrients essential to plants can be applied in a variety of methods as well.

19
00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:18.397
As the crops grow, they are vulnerable to many pests, such as bacteria, insects, rodents, and other animals.

20
00:02:18.398 --> 00:02:25.690
Chemical companies also hoped to create pesticides that would reduce the threat from these pests, allowing the crops to mature.

21
00:02:25.691 --> 00:02:36.621
A breakthrough occurred in 1939; Swiss chemist Paul Müller found that a chemical compound called DDT was able to kill insects easily.

22
00:02:36.622 --> 00:02:41.674
Around the world, chemical companies began selling it to farmers in great quantities.

23
00:02:41.675 --> 00:02:52.104
By 1945, most farms in the United States and Great Britain were relying on the chemical to protect their crops from insects and ensure high yields.

24
00:02:52.105 --> 00:03:00.050
The benefits were undeniable; farmers were able to grow more food to feed the world's population, which was increasing rapidly.

25
00:03:00.051 --> 00:03:06.819
The Green Revolution prevented the starvation of billions of people who would not otherwise have had enough food to eat.

26
00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:08.914
After the Green Revolution

27
00:03:08.915 --> 00:03:15.530
By the 1960s, people began to notice certain negative side effects caused by these techniques.

28
00:03:15.531 --> 00:03:26.460
Although the Green Revolution allowed farmers to keep pace with population growth, it did so at the expense of the environment, safety to humans, and the overall quality of food.

29
00:03:26.461 --> 00:03:35.890
The organic movement gained popularity when it was reported that the widespread use of artificial fertilizers and pesticides was damaging the environment.

30
00:03:35.891 --> 00:03:41.786
Water quality deteriorates when farmers overuse artificial fertilizers and pesticides.

31
00:03:41.787 --> 00:03:50.827
This is because the excess chemicals leak through the soil into the underground water system or are flushed into nearby ponds and creeks.

32
00:03:50.828 --> 00:03:58.177
This leads to high levels of nitrogen, potassium, and other chemicals that are poisonous to living creatures in nearby bodies of water.

33
00:03:58.178 --> 00:04:02.919
Animals that live in these water sources or get their food from them suffer.

34
00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:10.601
For example, the concentration of DDT ingested by bald eagles through the fish they eat prevents them from laying healthy eggs.

35
00:04:10.602 --> 00:04:21.818
So, in the 1950s and 1960s, the eggs would hatch before their time, and the hatchlings died, causing a serious drop in the bald eagle population in the U.S.

36
00:04:21.819 --> 00:04:27.959
It was also suggested that the use of artificial chemicals was related to certain problems with human health.

37
00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:40.011
People exposed to high levels of pesticides, such as the farmers who apply them, have higher rates of cancer and birth defects as well as more problems with their respiratory systems, skin, and memory.

38
00:04:40.012 --> 00:04:51.952
But it is possible to be exposed to these chemicals outside of the fields; chemical residues often remain on the fruit and vegetables bought in stores, posing health concerns for people.

39
00:04:51.953 --> 00:05:01.392
One study done in the U.S. showed that over 70% of the fruit found in stores contains at least one type of pesticide, and sometimes as many as forty.

40
00:05:01.393 --> 00:05:07.836
Consumers have also noted that the quality and taste of foods grown with artificial chemicals have changed for the worse.

41
00:05:07.837 --> 00:05:11.368
A good-looking product appeals to consumers in the supermarket.

42
00:05:11.369 --> 00:05:18.220
Tomatoes, for instance, are grown to have a thick skin so they don't bruise easily when in transport or storage.

43
00:05:18.221 --> 00:05:22.552
Unfortunately, this has the side effect of diminishing the tomatoes' flavor.

44
00:05:22.553 --> 00:05:31.195
Furthermore, crops grown with artificial chemicals have a lower nutritional content because most fertilizers are made of only a few key compounds.

45
00:05:31.196 --> 00:05:37.688
As a result, the plants contain far fewer of the many minerals and vitamins that humans need in their diets.

46
00:05:37.689 --> 00:05:42.799
For example, tomatoes that are not organically grown are much lower in Vitamin C.

47
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:44.808
The Organic Foods Trend

48
00:05:44.809 --> 00:05:53.174
With so many reasons to avoid fruits and vegetables that are grown with artificial chemicals, it is no wonder that people have started buying organic foods.

49
00:05:53.175 --> 00:05:59.547
But how can farmers grow enough food for billions of people and not rely on artificial fertilizers and pesticides?

50
00:05:59.548 --> 00:06:06.836
Organic farmers understand that healthy plants need healthy soil, so they concentrate on making sure the soil gets enough nutrients.

51
00:06:06.837 --> 00:06:16.608
One way to do this is to follow the example of ancient farmers by making a natural fertilizer out of compost, a mixture of decomposing vegetable matter.

52
00:06:16.609 --> 00:06:23.694
Farmers can easily make compost by collecting the stems and other leftover parts of plants that are not used for food.

53
00:06:23.695 --> 00:06:30.082
Then, they put this plant material into a container where it can rot until it is ready to be used.

54
00:06:30.083 --> 00:06:37.689
Using compost provides essential nutrients for the fields and uses up all the waste from the crops, a type of recycling.

55
00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:44.756
Farmers can also use compost as mulch, which is a layer of material that is spread on top of the soil.

56
00:06:44.757 --> 00:06:54.408
Mulch made from grass clippings, straw, or bark chips can provide moisture for soil in dry climates and protect the fields from wind erosion.

57
00:06:54.409 --> 00:07:02.178
Most importantly, farmers must give soil enough time between harvests to regain its nutritional content and strength.

58
00:07:02.179 --> 00:07:08.120
If they can't let their fields lie unused and rest, they can practice crop rotation.

59
00:07:08.121 --> 00:07:19.273
Farmers who rotate their crops alternate which plants they grow on the same land, as opposed to growing the same crop in the same place season after season, which eventually depletes the soil's nutrients.

60
00:07:19.274 --> 00:07:25.501
Barley, for instance, requires a lot of nitrogen, but beans can replace nitrogen in the soil.

61
00:07:25.502 --> 00:07:32.390
If farmers alternate between barley and beans on the same field, they can maintain balanced and healthy soil.

62
00:07:32.391 --> 00:07:37.807
Of course, using organic methods is not as easy as spraying a field with artificial chemicals.

63
00:07:37.808 --> 00:07:45.755
It's a time-consuming and expensive process, and farmers must be knowledgeable about how to manage their crops using organic methods.

64
00:07:45.756 --> 00:07:50.794
Consequently, organic foods tend to be more expensive than nonorganic foods.

65
00:07:50.795 --> 00:07:59.334
For many people, this is a reasonable price to pay for healthy produce that tastes great and is good for the environment and the people in it.

